§ `VerifyWrittenGuardsFixture`

-- Negative controls: conditions the drafter DID write that exclude nothing
-- (smucclaw/l4-ide#1093). Each is reported once, as a `vacuous-guard`, at the
-- place it sits in the rule as written.
--
-- verify-vacuous-guard.l4 has the plainest case, `x AND (x OR y)`, whose
-- normal form is the rule itself. These are the cases where the normal form
-- and the rule as written come apart.

-- (1) The second condition is redundant, and its normal form is two clauses,
--     `x OR q` and `x OR r`. One finding, at the condition, not one per clause.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN, q IS A BOOLEAN, r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in two parts` x q r IF
    x AND (x OR (q AND r))

-- (2) The redundant condition is inside one limb of a disjunction. The rule
--     as written has no conjunction at the top; its normal form does.
GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN, q IS A BOOLEAN, r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle inside a limb` p q r IF
       (p AND (p OR q))
    OR r

-- (3) The redundant condition is inside the requirement of a rule with a
--     scope, which is read in the context of that scope.
GIVEN s IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in the requirement` s x y IF
    s IMPLIES (x AND (x OR y))

-- (4) The redundant condition is in what a row of a BRANCH says, which is
--     checked like any other condition the drafter wrote.
GIVEN s IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in a row` s x y IF
  BRANCH IF s THEN x AND (x OR y)
         OTHERWISE FALSE
